    "## 2. Race selection\n",
    "\n",
    "### Scope constraint (assignment-mandated)\n",
    "The assignment specifies the 1950–2020 Kaggle dataset. `year <= 2020` is applied **before any ranking**, directly from the `races.csv` `year` column.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Candidate pool\n",
    "`tire_age` needs pit-stop data, so candidates are further restricted to `raceId`s present in **both** `lap_times.csv` and `pit_stops.csv` — pit-stop records only exist from the 2011 season onward in this dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Driver-count requirement — correct interpretation\n",
    "The assignment asks for a race with **5–10 drivers who completed it**, i.e. the analysis uses a 5–10-driver subset of a race's classified finishers — not a race whose *entire* finishing field is 5–10 cars. A candidate race must have **≥5 classified finishers with ≥1 pit stop** (≥2 stints, the minimum for an earlier-stints-train / final-stint-test split), and — under the leak-free cleaning rule defined in Section 3 — each such driver must still have a non-empty train partition and non-empty test partition *after* cleaning.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Dry / no-red-flag screening — heuristic, not proof, and not a leakage concern\n",
    "None of the four provided CSVs contains a weather, red-flag, or race-interruption field. In the absence of a trustworthy field, the following heuristic screens candidate **races** (it does not touch the eventual train/test split, and no split exists yet at this stage — so there is no test-set information for it to leak into):\n",
    "- Exclude each driver's pit-in and pit-out-adjacent laps.\n",
    "- Flag a lap as anomalous for a driver if it exceeds 1.5× that driver's own field-wide median (a proxy for safety car / VSC / rain / red-flag restarts).\n",
    "- `mass_events` = number of laps where many drivers are anomalous **simultaneously** (a field-wide-incident signature).\n",
    "- `cv` = **coefficient of variation of the field's per-driver median lap times**: the standard deviation of `{driver 1's median lap time, driver 2's median lap time, ...}` divided by the **mean** of that same set. Lower means the drivers' typical pace was more uniform across the field — one signal (not proof) of a clean, incident-free race. (A std/mean ratio computed on the same population is what makes this a coefficient of variation at all; std divided by a different population's median would not be.)\n",
    "\n",
    "Candidates are ranked by `mass_events` ascending, then `cv` ascending; the top-ranked race is selected. This does not *prove* the race was dry and incident-free — it identifies the most internally-consistent candidate the available data can support."
   ]
  },
